// pages-wrangler.jsonc — Cloudflare PAGES project configuration for the QA authentication bridge.
//
// This is a TEMPLATE, not a config Wrangler reads from here. `node infra/qa-auth-bridge/
// pages-build.ts` copies it verbatim to `dist-qa-auth-pages/wrangler.jsonc`, and the Pages
// commands are run from that directory.
//
// Why it cannot live at the repository root, verified against the installed Wrangler 4.131.1:
//   - `wrangler pages dev|deploy -c <path>` is REFUSED outright — "Pages does not support custom
//     paths for the Wrangler configuration file. Remove the --config flag, or use a standard
//     wrangler.jsonc in your project root."
//   - With no `-c`, the Pages commands read the repository root `wrangler.jsonc`, which is the
//     PRODUCTION Worker (`quickserve`, assets-only, `./dist`). Running them at the root therefore
//     mixes the Production Worker's configuration — and the root `.env` — into a QA Pages session.
//
// So the build writes a self-contained deployment workspace instead:
//
//     dist-qa-auth-pages/
//       wrangler.jsonc     ← this file, copied; the only config Wrangler finds from there
//       origin/            ← the uploaded build output: _worker.js + the certified assets
//
// `wrangler.jsonc` sits OUTSIDE `origin/`, so it is never uploaded as an asset, and Wrangler stops
// its upward config search at the workspace, so neither the Production Worker config nor the
// repository `.env` is read.
//
// Advanced mode: `origin/_worker.js` is one self-contained ES module bundled from
// infra/qa-auth-bridge/worker.ts — the same module the live Workers target deploys. Pages routes
// every request to it (the equivalent of the Workers target's `run_worker_first: true`) and it
// reaches the assets through the default `env.ASSETS` binding.
//
// Deliberately absent: `account_id` (supply CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID at deploy time), every secret,
// every route, the custom domain, and `BRIDGE_MODE`. On Pages, `BRIDGE_MODE` is a project
// environment variable; unset serves the bridge, so the deny-all rollback is applied by setting it
// to anything other than "serve" on the project itself.
//
// Deploying this target is a SEPARATE, individually authorised gate. See ./README.md.
{
  "name": "kwikserve-auth-qa-bridge",
  "pages_build_output_dir": "./origin",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-09-01"
}
